One date format `package:lubridate` doesn't seem to support directly is unix timestamps (seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00).  

A helper along the lines of:

```
#' @examples
#' as.timestamp("1970-01-02") == 86400
#' as.timestamp("1970-01-02 00:00:00") == 86400
#' as.timestamp(days(1))
setGeneric("as.timestamp",def = function(time) {
  stop("input must be a vector of character, POSIXct, or integer64")
})
setMethod("as.timestamp", signature = "integer64", function(time) {
  return(time)
})
setMethod("as.timestamp", signature = "numeric", function(time) {
  return(time)
})
setMethod("as.timestamp", signature = "Date", function(time) {
  return(as.numeric(difftime(time,as.Date("1970-01-01"),units="secs") %>% as.integer()))
})
setMethod("as.timestamp", signature = "POSIXct", function(time) {
  return(as.numeric(difftime(time,as.Date("1970-01-01"),units="secs") %>% as.integer()))
})
setMethod("as.timestamp", signature = "character", function(time) {
  return(as.numeric(difftime(as.POSIXct(time),as.Date("1970-01-01"),units="secs") %>% as.integer()))
})
setMethod("as.timestamp", signature("Period"), function(time) {
  as.numeric(difftime(ymd("2000-01-01")+time, ymd("2000-01-01"), units = "secs"))
})
```

Seems potentially useful.  ... obviously this isn't written in a way that takes advantage of the idioms in lubridate - the code above is merely illustrative.

Does that fall in the scope of things you'd like to see lubridate able to handle/do?

